cartridge "CUT-OFF!/GAZETTE/1984"

' CUT-OFF!
' A ColecoVision game based on "Cut-Off!", COMPUTE!'s Gazette March 1984
' (Commodore 64 and VIC-20 machine language; no author is named on the
' disk, which a reader retyped around 1988; the C64 and VIC-20 programs
' agree on every rule and number checked).
' Made for Amy Studio by Claude (an AI made by Anthropic), 2026, in Amy by
' Amy Bienvenu.
'
' Not a cell-for-cell port: the rules and numbers are the original's (read
' from the C64 machine code, orig/c64.dis, and watched in jsc64), rebuilt
' for the ColecoVision: heads that glide from cell to cell, joined light
' trails, sound.
'
' The game: two players each steer a line that never stops growing. Run
' into a wall, a trail (your own too) or the other head and you crash.
'
' From the original (orig/c64.dis): the lines start facing each other on
' the middle row, blue moving right, red moving left. Every step the step
' counter goes up by one, blue moves first, then red. A stick held in one
' of the four directions turns the line (diagonals and a centred stick
' keep the direction; turning straight back runs into your own trail).
' A head moving into any cell that is not empty crashes: the OTHER player
' scores the round's step count and the crasher loses one of 10 lives.
' A crash into the other head when the two were moving straight at each
' other is a head-on: both score the step count and both lose a life.
' The game ends when a player has no lives left. Skill 0-9 picked with
' up/down before the game; a button pauses and resumes. After a round:
' PRESS BUTTON TO CONTINUE with both players' lives.
'
' Liberties: the arena is 26 x 18 cells (the C64's was 38 x 23); the
' heads start symmetrically (columns 8 and 23 of the screen, the C64 used
' 7 and 17 of 40); the step time per skill is 12 frames at 0 down to 3 at
' 9 (the C64's delay loops gave about 11 frames at 0, 6 at 5 and one frame
' at 9, too fast for people); a direction pressed between two steps is
' remembered for the next step (the C64 read the stick only at the step);
' a one-player game against the computer (red), which keeps straight
' while it has room, turns now and then at random, and turns towards the
' longer free line when blocked; the higher score is named the winner at
' the end (the C64 just showed GAME OVER and the scores); the heads glide
' between cells and the trails are drawn joined; a crash burst instead of
' the C64's border flash; PAUSE shown while paused.
'
' Controls: controller 1 steers blue, controller 2 red (in a one-player
' game the computer steers red). Either button pauses. On the title:
' left/right picks 1 or 2 players, up/down the skill, a button starts.
'
' House rules: text in columns 2-29, rows 1-22; tiles from code 128; the
' frame loop is protected from the NMI; screens drawn with the NMI off
' after a wait, display off. Music first in the sound table, effects after;
' the title tune is played once.

